// Canonical Orna text is schema directed. Decoding accepts the data subset
// for the supplied type only; it never executes declarations, imports,
// interpolations or arbitrary functions. Refinements are revalidated before
// a value is returned. Unsupported or private data fails instead of being
// omitted or converted through a presentation string.
//
// Encoded output is UTF-8 without a BOM, uses LF, two-space record indentation,
// one space after `:`, comma-separated array and tuple items, a trailing comma
// after each record field, and exactly one final LF. Record order follows the
// primary-key order followed by stable field-ID order for rows, and NFC field
// name order for structural records. Arrays and tuples retain value order.
// Int uses minimal decimal digits; Decimal uses its normalised coefficient
// and exponent; finite Float uses 17 significant digits and preserves signed
// zero. String controls use the canonical escapes. Blob values use quoted,
// padded standard Base64. Locale formatting is never a serialization form.
// Decode calls supply the required static type witness using the call form
// `decode(input, as: T)`; `as` selects T and is not a runtime value argument.

pub fn encode<T>(value: T): Str = fail(error(
    code: "ORNA-EVAL-UNSUPPORTED",
    message: "std.encoding.orna.encode requires its canonical text codec binding"
));

pub fn decode<T>(input: Str): T = fail(error(
    code: "ORNA-EVAL-UNSUPPORTED",
    message: "std.encoding.orna.decode requires its schema-directed codec binding"
));
